<Base title="Breadth-First Search · ITC 120" description="Breadth-first search (BFS) in plain English: visiting a graph level by level with a queue, shortest paths, and connectivity." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Midterm › Graphs</p>
    <p class="kicker">Graphs · Lesson 2 of 3</p>
    <h1>Breadth-First Search (BFS)</h1>
    <KindChips />
    <p class="lede">Visit a graph in waves: first the start, then everything one step away, then two steps away, and so on.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>explain what a <b>graph traversal</b> is and why it needs a "seen" list;</li>
        <li>run BFS by hand with a <b>queue</b> (neighbors in alphabetical order) and write the BFS order, for undirected and directed graphs;</li>
        <li>give each vertex's <b>level</b> and recover a <b>shortest path</b> (fewest edges) from the start using the "found by" table;</li>
        <li>use BFS to tell whether an undirected graph is <b>connected</b>, count its pieces, and list what a directed graph can reach;</li>
        <li>match each line of a Java BFS to the steps of the procedure.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the <a href={base + '/itc120/graphs/'}>Graphs lesson</a>, the <a href={base + '/itc120/queues/'}>Queues lesson</a>, and scratch paper.</p>
  </header>

  <!-- 1 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="idea">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The idea</p>
      <h2>Exploring in waves</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Graph traversal</p>
        <p>A <b>graph traversal</b> visits every vertex that can be reached from a starting vertex, each one <b>exactly once</b>. There are two main types: breadth-first search (this lesson) and depth-first search (next lesson).</p>
      </div>
      <p>For trees, you learned pre-order, in-order, and post-order. Graphs need something new: they have no root and can have <b>cycles</b>, so if we are not careful, we could go around a cycle forever. The fix is to remember which vertices we have already <b>seen</b>.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={treeVsGraph()} /><figcaption>A tree has one root and no cycles, so every path down ends. A graph can have a cycle: A, B, C, D, and back to A. Without a "seen" list, a traversal could go around forever. With one, it skips A the second time, so each vertex is visited once.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Breadth-first search (BFS)</p>
        <p><b>Breadth-first search (BFS)</b> is a <b>graph traversal algorithm</b> that visits every vertex reachable from a starting vertex, <b>nearest first</b>: the start, then every vertex 1 edge away, then every vertex 2 edges away, and so on. This is called going <b>level by level</b>. BFS uses a <b>queue</b> to keep track of which vertex to visit next.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Level</p>
        <p>A vertex's <b>level</b> is how many edges away it is from the start. The start itself is <b>level 0</b> (0 edges away). Its neighbors are level 1, their new neighbors are level 2, and so on. In this course, the start is always level 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Good to know: some books start at 1</p>
        <p>Some textbooks count levels from <b>1</b> instead, mostly for trees: the root is level 1, its children are level 2, and so on. It is the same picture; only the numbers move up by one. If a quiz or a book from somewhere else says "level", check which way it counts. "Number of edges from the start" (also called the <b>distance</b>) always starts at 0, so it never changes.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={wavesSvg} /><figcaption>The graph you will use in Part 3, sorted into levels from A. A level is how many edges away a vertex is from the start. BFS visits all of level 1 before any of level 2, and the queue holds the vertices in exactly that order.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="sticky">
        <p class="note-label">Think of news spreading in a barangay</p>
        <p>You tell the news to your next-door neighbors first. Then each of them tells <i>their</i> neighbors. Then those people tell theirs. The news spreads outward in waves: everyone 1 house away hears it before anyone 2 houses away. That is BFS.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={newsWaves()} /><figcaption>Each number is the house's level. You start with the news, so you are level 0. Orange arrows: you tell your next-door neighbors (level 1, the first wave). Amber arrows: one of them tells its own neighbors (level 2). The corner houses hear it last (level 3). Here the houses are the vertices, and "next door" is an edge.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Where you see BFS in IT</p>
        <p><b>"People you may know"</b> suggestions often come from level 2: friends of your friends. In <b>networks</b>, BFS can find the route with the fewest hops between two computers.</p>
        <p>A <b>web crawler</b> is a program that search engines use to find web pages by following links. It can go level by level, like BFS: first the pages 1 link away, then 2 links away.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={bfsInIT()} /><figcaption>Three places BFS shows up. Friend suggestions come from level 2 (people, with friendships as edges). Network routes count hops (devices, with links as edges). A crawler collects pages level by level (pages, with links as edges).</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="queue">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · The tool</p>
      <h2>Why a queue?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Recall the queue from the linear data structures lessons: you <b>enqueue</b> at the rear and <b>dequeue</b> from the front, like a line at the canteen. First in, first out.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={canteenLine()} /><figcaption>A canteen line is a queue. Student 1 came first, so student 1 is served first and leaves from the front (<b>dequeue</b>). A new student joins at the rear (<b>enqueue</b>), so the new student waits for students 1 to 4.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>BFS puts every newly found vertex at the <b>rear</b> of the queue. Since all level-1 vertices are found before any level-2 vertex, they are also in line first. So the queue automatically keeps the waves in order: <span class="hl">closer vertices are always visited before farther ones</span>.</p>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Seen list</p>
        <p>A <b>seen list</b> is a checklist of every vertex BFS has already found. A vertex gets its ✓ the moment it joins the queue, and a vertex with a ✓ is never added again. That is what stops BFS from going around a cycle forever (<Peek label="Part 1" title="From Part 1" html={cyclePeek} />).</p>
        <p><b>Seen is not the same as visited.</b> A vertex is <b>seen</b> when it joins the queue, and <b>visited</b> later, when it leaves the front.</p>
      </div>
      <StepShow id="bfs-queue" cap="One queue, step by step" hint="BFS uses only one queue. Tap Next and watch it: a vertex leaves from the front, new ones join at the rear." steps={queueSteps()} />
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The BFS procedure</h3>
        <p>Every BFS follows these steps:</p>
        <ol class="procedure">
          <li>Mark the start vertex as <b>seen</b> (give it a ✓ on the seen list) and <b>enqueue</b> it.</li>
          <li>While the queue is not empty:
            <ol type="a">
              <li><b>Dequeue</b> the vertex at the front. <b>Visit</b> it (write it in the BFS order).</li>
              <li>Look at its neighbors, in alphabetical order (or smallest number first, if the vertices are numbers). For each one that is <b>not seen</b> yet (no ✓): give it a ✓ and <b>enqueue</b> it. Skip the ones that already have a ✓.</li>
            </ol>
          </li>
          <li>When the queue is empty, BFS is done.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Exam traps</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Mark a vertex as seen <b>when you enqueue it</b>, not when you dequeue it. Otherwise it can enter the queue twice.</li>
          <li>When a vertex has several unseen neighbors, the order you enqueue them changes the answer. Any order is still a correct BFS (the levels stay the same), so if the question does not say, more than one BFS order can be correct. <b>Alphabetical order</b> is the usual safe choice; for numbered vertices, use <b>smallest number first</b>, comparing them as numbers (2 before 10). Just be consistent. In code, the order is however the neighbors are stored.</li>
          <li>In a directed graph, follow the arrows only. An arrow <i>into</i> a vertex does not let you leave through it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="plain">
        <p class="note-label">Good to know: why "alphabetical order"?</p>
        <p>BFS itself does not say which neighbor comes first, so one graph can have several correct BFS orders. Many books, courses, and exams add a rule, usually alphabetical order (or smallest number first), for one reason: <b>so everyone gets the same answer</b>. Then answers are easy to check and compare. An order is only wrong if it breaks the BFS steps or the rule the question gives. In a program, there is no alphabet: the code takes the neighbors in the order they are stored.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={bfsOrders()} /><figcaption>Each box is colored by its level. Both correct orders go level by level: all of level 1 before any of level 2. They differ only in which neighbor goes first, and C first also puts C's neighbor E first. The wrong order jumps to D (level 2) while C (level 1) is still waiting.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="lab">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Watch it</p>
      <h2>BFS, step by step</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>In Part 2 you watched BFS from A. Now it is your turn to predict: this lab starts at <b>D</b>. Before each tap of <b>Next step</b>, say out loud which vertex leaves the queue next and which ones join. Then pick other starting vertices with the round buttons.</p>
      <TraverseLab id="bfs-lab" mode="bfs" cap="BFS lab" hint="Orange = being visited now. Light teal = seen, waiting in the queue. Dark teal = visited. The small L-numbers are levels." presets={[
        { label: 'Main example', g: city, start: 'D' },
        { label: 'Directed graph', g: oneWay, start: 'A' },
        { label: 'Separate pieces', g: islands, start: 'A' },
      ]} />
      <p>Whatever start you pick, the order follows the levels. From A (Part 2), the BFS order was <b>{cityRun.order.join(', ')}</b>: A (level 0), then B and D (level 1), then C and E (level 2), then F and G (level 3).</p>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The other two graphs</h3>
        <p>The lab also has two other graphs. They are <b>not</b> different kinds of BFS: the steps are exactly the same. They only show what BFS does on other kinds of graphs:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Directed graph:</b> the edges are arrows (<Peek label="from the Graphs lesson" title="From the Graphs lesson" html={dirPeek} />). BFS can only follow an arrow forward. From A, BFS never reaches F, because F's only arrow points away from F.</li>
          <li><b>Separate pieces:</b> the graph is in three parts with no edges between them. From A, BFS stops after A, B, and C, because no edge leads to the other parts. Part 5 uses this.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="levels">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Bonus from BFS</p>
      <h2>Levels and shortest paths</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>BFS gives you more than a visit order. While it runs, it also answers a very common question: <b>what is the fewest number of steps from the start to each vertex?</b></p>
      <p>You saw this question in <Peek label="Part 1" title="From Part 1" html={itPeek} />: the route with the <b>fewest hops</b> between two computers, and "people you may know" (people <b>2 friendships</b> away). Another example: the fewest links you must click to get from one web page to another. When every edge counts the same (an unweighted graph), "fewest steps" means "fewest edges".</p>
      <p>BFS gets this answer for free because of the waves. It reaches every level-1 vertex before any level-2 vertex, and so on, so the first time BFS finds a vertex, it found it the shortest way. That is why a vertex's <b>level</b> is exactly the <b>fewest edges</b> needed to reach it from the start.</p>
      <figure class="fig">
        <p class="fig-cap">Fewest edges from A to G</p>
        <GraphFig g={city} labels={levelLabels} edgeMarks={pathMarks} nodeMarks={pathNodes} label="Main example with levels; the path A, B, E, G is highlighted" />
        <p class="small">Each L-number is the vertex's level. G is at level 3, so a shortest path from A to G uses 3 edges: <b>{pathG.join(' → ')}</b>.</p>
      </figure>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>How to recover the path</h3>
        <p>The level tells you <b>how far</b> G is (3 edges). To also know <b>which way</b> to go, write down <b>who found each vertex</b> while doing BFS (its parent in the BFS). Then start at G and follow the parents back to A:</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Vertex</th>{Object.keys(cityRun.parent).sort().map((k) => <th>{k}</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td><b>Found by</b></td>{Object.keys(cityRun.parent).sort().map((k) => <td>{cityRun.parent[k]}</td>)}</tr></tbody>
        </table>
        <p>G was found by E, E by B, B by A. Reversed: A → B → E → G.</p>
        <p>A → D → E → G also uses 3 edges, so there are two shortest paths. BFS gives the one through B because B left the queue before D and reached E first. On an exam, any path with the fewest edges is correct unless the question asks for the BFS path.</p>
        <p>The vertex with the highest level is the one <b>farthest</b> from the start (here, F and G, at level 3).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Careful</p>
        <p>"Shortest" here means <b>fewest edges</b>. In a <b>weighted</b> graph, the path with fewer edges is not always the cheapest one For example, in the <Peek label="travel-time graph" title="From the Graphs lesson" html={travelPeek} /> from the Graphs lesson, A → B → C takes 19 minutes. If there were also a direct A–C road taking 30 minutes, A → C would have fewer edges but take 11 minutes longer. Weighted shortest paths use other algorithms, such as Dijkstra's algorithm.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="connected">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Bonus from BFS</p>
      <h2>Is everything connected?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>BFS visits <b>everything reachable</b> from the start, and nothing else. That makes it a quick test:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Undirected graph:</b> run BFS from any vertex. If every vertex gets visited, the graph is <b>connected</b>. If not, it is in separate pieces.</li>
        <li><b>Directed graph:</b> BFS from S tells you exactly which vertices <b>can be reached from S</b> by following arrows.</li>
      </ul>
      <figure class="fig">
        <p class="fig-cap">Separate pieces</p>
        <GraphFig g={islands} label="Graph in three separate pieces: A, B, C; D, E, F; and G alone" />
        <p class="small">BFS from A visits only A, B, C. To count the pieces, start a new BFS from any vertex not yet visited (D visits D, E, F; then G alone). Three BFS runs = <b>3 pieces</b>. Try "Separate pieces" in the lab above.</p>
      </figure>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">In IT</p>
        <p>This is how a network tool can check whether every computer in an office can still reach the server after a cable is unplugged.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="paper">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 6 · Your turn</p>
      <h2>Try it on paper</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">How to set up your paper</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Draw the queue as a row of boxes. When you dequeue, cross out the front box. Add new vertices at the right end.</li>
          <li>Keep a "seen" list beside it, and write the BFS order underneath.</li>
          <li>Write each vertex's level next to it on the drawing.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Problem 1</p>
        <GraphFig g={p1} label="Practice graph 1, undirected, vertices A to G" />
        <ol>
          <li>Do a BFS starting at <b>D</b> (neighbors in alphabetical order). Write the BFS order.</li>
          <li>Give the level of every vertex.</li>
          <li>Which vertex or vertices are farthest from D?</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="reveal">
          <summary>Show the answer</summary>
          <div class="flow">
            <p><b>BFS order:</b> <span class="mono">{r1.order.join(', ')}</span></p>
            <table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Vertices</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>{r1Levels.map((l) => <tr><td>{l.level}</td><td>{l.nodes.join(', ')}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
            <p><b>Farthest:</b> {r1Max.nodes.join(' and ')} (level {r1Max.level}). D's neighbors B, C, F, G all go in the queue first; A and E are found only on the second wave.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Problem 2 (directed)</p>
        <GraphFig g={p2} label="Practice graph 2, directed, vertices A to F" />
        <ol>
          <li>Do a BFS starting at <b>A</b>, following the arrows. Write the BFS order.</li>
          <li>Which vertex is never visited? Why?</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="reveal">
          <summary>Show the answer</summary>
          <div class="flow">
            <p><b>BFS order:</b> <span class="mono">{r2.order.join(', ')}</span></p>
            <table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Vertices</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>{r2Levels.map((l) => <tr><td>{l.level}</td><td>{l.nodes.join(', ')}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
            <p><b>Never visited:</b> {r2.missed.join(', ')}. F has an arrow <i>to</i> A, but no arrow comes <i>into</i> F, so there is no way to reach F from A. When E looks at C, C is already seen, so it is skipped.</p>
